<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.16.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01148</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.16.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>671f1e4a-bccc-44aa-86ab-ef3c571d3b69</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Ask students to discuss with a partner: “How are arithmetic and geometric sequences alike and different?” After they have had a few minutes to discuss, ask several students to share the things that came up. Some things to highlight:</para>
<list>
  <item>
    They are both types of sequences. That is, they are both lists of numbers.
  </item>
  <item>
    To get from one term to the next for both arithmetic and geometric sequences, you “do the same thing each time.”</item>
  <item>
    For geometric sequences, you always multiply by the same number to get the next term. For arithmetic sequences, you always add the same number to get the next term.
  </item>
  <item>
    Geometric sequences have growth factors (the quotient of any term and the previous term), but arithmetic sequences have a constant rate of change (the difference of any term and the previous term).
  </item>
</list></section>
</content>
